{"title":"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the north pole. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are points on a great circle through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> equidistant from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a point on the equator. Show that the great circle through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> bisects the angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/3/21361fc8f29984fa3e95bfc0e578efdbce08c736.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACB$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> in the spherical triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> (a spherical triangle has great circle arcs as sides).\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:USAMO_1979_P2a.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f4/USAMO_1979_P2a.png/500px-USAMO_1979_P2a.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"429\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f4/USAMO_1979_P2a.png/750px-USAMO_1979_P2a.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f4/USAMO_1979_P2a.png/1000px-USAMO_1979_P2a.png 2x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the north pole, we define the Earth with a sphere of radius one in space with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/5/3c5c8d8be2838dd513a8b0224398d4ef0f7d2478.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N=(0,0,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"100\" height=\"20\" /> and sphere center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/7/917f191ee61209a4958553a422b0e47289266459.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O=(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />\nWe then pick point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> on the sphere and define the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/d/2dd70347c7940f63ff9417e383548564fd92aff7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"10\" />-plane as the plane that contains great circle points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> , <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> with the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-axis perpendicular to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />-axis and in the direction of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Using this coordinate system and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> axes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/8/e88730150f1b7dc72a3956ed192bcedda14662c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=(cos(\\phi),0,sin(\\phi))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"180\" height=\"20\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is the angle from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/7/3f79c3676b62fb47243e0d357c87115b6f3395e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"13\" />-plane to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> or latitude on this sphere with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/1/fd13633601964ae45c474448e86454cb68257751.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{-\\pi}{2} &lt; \\phi &lt; \\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"105\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are points on a great circle through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> equidistant from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/2/bd2cc1aeef262c76112af1f7cbebcac28f2e5304.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B=(-cos(\\phi),0,sin(\\phi))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"195\" height=\"20\" /> \n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a point on the equator, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/9/c39167b66593467d40b69b7481a0017921a92671.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C=(cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"177\" height=\"20\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the angle on the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/7/3f79c3676b62fb47243e0d357c87115b6f3395e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"13\" />-plane from the origin to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> or longitude on this sphere with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/c/8dca16a23b5ebc83cfefdeb4c0a0dd318ac2c5b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\pi &lt; \\phi \\le \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We note that vectors from the origin to points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are all unit vectors because all those points are on the unit sphere.\n</p><p>So, we're going to define points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> as unit vectors with their coordinates.\n</p><p>We also define the following vectors as follows:\n</p><p>Vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5da51147655583f4143c450497e5eb44d39fda1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CN}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"24\" /> is the unit vector in the direction of arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/3/ba369b33271c6fb52789f86292cc68559a2e0411.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CN$\" width=\"31\" height=\"12\" /> and tangent to the great circle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/3/ba369b33271c6fb52789f86292cc68559a2e0411.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CN$\" width=\"31\" height=\"12\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/a/f1a6ba5f9887f169fc168d36cbd14459a9773a5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CA}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"24\" /> is the unit vector in the direction of arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e657259c0995d2c36d46744bb4aa81471e6575ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CA$\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" /> and tangent to the great circle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e657259c0995d2c36d46744bb4aa81471e6575ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CA$\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/0/540f8ff3b559973d3a76b07438fe29b144059b5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CB}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"24\" /> is the unit vector in the direction of arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/3/3e3a4ec0df571e719bd3f1d7e00bf71fc55ba2b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CB$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> and tangent to the great circle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/3/3e3a4ec0df571e719bd3f1d7e00bf71fc55ba2b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CB$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>To calculate each of these vectors we shall use the cross product as follows:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/c/45c4d6bd323e37ba40b8912339fd2656a18b6fea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CN}}=(\\overrightarrow{C}\\times\\overrightarrow{N})\\times\\overrightarrow{C}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"173\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/e/4ced0269b7e862c4e2b515f1a4ca61e817e7d2be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CN}}=(\\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle\\times\\left\\langle 0,0,1 \\right\\rangle)\\times \\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"449\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/0/1408d7196632fbb2730f53b0b4dce028fa4bdc48.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CN}}=\\left\\langle sin(\\theta),-cos(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle\\times \\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"369\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/e/8de1a14924f9e172c34f0f7ce4d91b55e376f992.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CN}}=\\left\\langle 0,0,sin^{2}(\\theta)+cos^{2}(\\theta) \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"245\" height=\"27\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/1/00118a6b171052802b1156ca25b71ac95e0c32ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CN}}=\\left\\langle 0,0,1 \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>Vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/a/f1a6ba5f9887f169fc168d36cbd14459a9773a5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CA}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"24\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/a/afa3d18faebd5394df1d1460baa00357684ceb17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CA}}=(\\overrightarrow{C}\\times\\overrightarrow{A})\\times\\overrightarrow{C}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"171\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/f/81f1f60d0418baf262d30c5fac344ad638e99211.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CA}}=(\\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle\\times\\left\\langle cos(\\phi),0,sin(\\phi) \\right\\rangle)\\times \\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"530\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/d/72d34c17ac01c92b43f5c3fa521855288e02ab95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CA}}=\\left\\langle sin(\\theta)sin(\\phi),-cos(\\theta)sin(\\phi),-sin(\\theta)cos(\\phi) \\right\\rangle\\times \\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"573\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>Since we're only interested in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> component of the vector\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/d/b8d018059c457e475c3123fe5de71c382db17588.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CA}}=\\left\\langle V_{CA_{x}},V_{CA_{y}},sin^{2}(\\theta)sin(\\phi)+cos^{2}(\\theta)sin(\\phi) \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"405\" height=\"27\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/d/ead245a001221ddfac0dba8957a9e7d8b9af2a0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CA}}=\\left\\langle V_{CA_{x}},V_{CA_{y}},sin(\\phi) \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"220\" height=\"27\" />\n</p><p>Vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/0/540f8ff3b559973d3a76b07438fe29b144059b5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CB}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"24\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/7/167e513dde90ae5b7d0e19e75e3be5d465e4a90e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CB}}=(\\overrightarrow{C}\\times\\overrightarrow{b})\\times\\overrightarrow{C}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"172\" height=\"26\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/1/0b1361f080ee27717ca24dd332885286401487f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CB}}=(\\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle\\times\\left\\langle -cos(\\phi),0,sin(\\phi) \\right\\rangle)\\times \\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"545\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/6/8463500323e547d86ad95285a7f48fceb4566a31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CB}}=\\left\\langle sin(\\theta)sin(\\phi),-cos(\\theta)sin(\\phi),sin(\\theta)cos(\\phi) \\right\\rangle\\times \\left\\langle cos(\\theta),sin(\\theta),0 \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"559\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>Since we're only interested in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> component of the vector\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/0/f407317b5023f60ad90c28235ff81db6c3488814.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CB}}=\\left\\langle V_{CB_{x}},V_{CB_{y}},sin^{2}(\\theta)sin(\\phi)+cos^{2}(\\theta)sin(\\phi) \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"405\" height=\"27\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/b/b6bf0567e048dae8d1e669bf8b2b3c7f1afcd12b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{V_{CB}}=\\left\\langle V_{CB_{x}},V_{CB_{y}},sin(\\phi) \\right\\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"220\" height=\"27\" />\n</p><p>Since we're working with unit vectors, then we can use dot products on the vectors with their angles as follows:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/d/49d248abe41bb911efaf2d2bd84282d656e6ee9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos(\\angle ACN) = \\overrightarrow{V_{CA}}\\cdot \\overrightarrow{V_{CN}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"201\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/d/fcd7747b7e4296818ec7c0d51d2c91fe26bdf2f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos(\\angle ACN) = \\left\\langle V_{CA_{x}},V_{CA_{y}},sin(\\phi) \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\left\\langle 0,0,1 \\right\\rangle = 0*V_{CA_{x}}+0*V_{CA_{y}}+1*sin(\\phi)=sin(\\phi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"706\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>Likewise,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/0/ea0e94ca63ee3c46b524b9809c942eb76435d695.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos(\\angle BCN) = \\overrightarrow{V_{CB}}\\cdot \\overrightarrow{V_{CN}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"203\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/7/787a7c6ca4f799943ce890c746f8e92fdef7e7aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos(\\angle BCN) = \\left\\langle V_{CB_{x}},V_{CB_{y}},sin(\\phi) \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\left\\langle 0,0,1 \\right\\rangle = 0*V_{CB_{x}}+0*V_{CB_{y}}+1*sin(\\phi)=sin(\\phi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"708\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>Therefore,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/5/c35b17f6b65772dd69b0e21eb6d6be6240b720cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos(\\angle ACN) = cos(\\angle BCN)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"221\" height=\"20\" /> and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/8/a78db7ea5f4cdf739b97eb077acbe928429f2eb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACN = \\angle BCN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"142\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Since those angles are equal, it proves that the great circle through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> bisects the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/0728bebb477a2636300c56851b6a6f9e382c1b83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> in the spherical triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> \n</p><p>~Tomas Diaz.  orders@tomasdiaz.com\n</p><p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO\" title=\"1979 USAMO\">1979 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1979\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017131558\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.048 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.082 seconds\nPreprocessor v